{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 7, :comments ["<b>From Aram Balak brought me… from the mountains of the east.</b> From the east. And he says, 'Come, curse Jacob for me.' Rashi, of blessed memory, explained: how foolish he is — he uprooted me from my place in order to curse the people called 'the portion of HaShem,' and were it not for them, we would never have come into the world. For in the merit of Abraham came Balak king of Moab from the seed of Lot, as it is said (Genesis 19:29): ״ויהי בשחת אלהים את ערי הככר ויזכור אלהים את אברהם וישלח את לוט״; and in the merit of Jacob came I, for Laban had no sons at first but only daughters — for Rachel was tending the flock, and once Jacob came to him, he merited sons, as it is said (Genesis 31:1): ״וישמע את דברי בני לבן״, and likewise Laban said (Genesis 30:27): ״נחשתי ויברכני ה׳ בגללך״ — and you say to me to curse them both? It turns out that both of us are ingrates." "<b>Come, curse Jacob for me.</b> He mentioned both names, Jacob and Israel; from this we learn that one who wishes to curse a person must curse him by all his names, and must curse him both with the attribute of day and with the attribute of night. And this is why he immediately juxtaposed: ״מה אקוב לא קבה אל״ — and he did not say זעם, in the manner of what is written (Psalms 7:12): ״ואל זועם בכל יום״; rather, he intended to mention the two attributes, and this is the language of Job (Job 3:3): ״יקבוהו אוררי יום״. But that he mentioned (Job 3:4): ״יאבד יום והלילה״ — in this his intention was evil, to deny the root-principle, and concerning this the Sages, of blessed memory, expounded: 'he stoned the icon — he cursed the Sovereign.' And likewise he would need to see him with his eyes, for it helps greatly for his curse to take hold upon him at the moment of looking; because of this Balak brought Balaam up to the high places each and every time. And in the matter of blessing too, when they wish to bless him, one must mention his names, in the manner of what is written: ״מה טובו אהליך יעקב משכנותיך ישראל״. And the expression ״ארה לי״ — he should have said 'ארה את יעקב,' but Balaam was saying thus: behold, Balak is saying to curse myself, since he says to curse Jacob. And so they said in the Midrash: Balaam said to him, 'whoever curses these, upon himself he curses,' for thus it is written (Genesis 12:3): ״ומקללך אאור״. And likewise every expression of cursing that Balak uttered against Israel each and every time, he would turn back upon himself: ארה לי, קבה לי, קבנו לי, וקבותו לי."], :book :numbers, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 23, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:35:25.711756866Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}